# The model, in the order it has to be applied.
#
# One list, read by migrate.sh and by verify.sh, so that the database a check runs against
# and the database that gets deployed are built from the same thing in the same order. Two
# lists would agree right up until the day they didn't.
#
# roles.sql is not here. It needs a role that may create roles and the rest of this does
# not, so it is a separate, deliberate step - see migrate.sh --with-roles.

schema.sql
agreements.sql
incidents.sql
authentication.sql
access-policies.sql
identity-policies.sql
emergency-and-program.sql
data-classification.sql
access-matrix.sql
audit-logging.sql
retention.sql
environments.sql
vendors.sql
backup-recovery.sql
phi-safe-logging.sql
encryption-and-secrets.sql
boundary.sql
gcp-iam.sql
grants.sql
schema-privileges.sql

# Appended, not inserted. schema_migrations.position is the primary key and a database that
# has already run the model holds positions 1..20, so a file slipped into the middle of this
# list claims a position another file is recorded under and the migration fails on a
# duplicate key. Forward changes go at the end.
#
# After schema-privileges.sql, which the migration checks name as the last thing applied.
# That check asks whoever appends past it to say why their file may run without CREATE on
# the schema: this one alters a table it already owns and creates a policy, neither of which
# needs it.
care-history.sql
audit-attribution.sql
family-access.sql
contact-acceptance.sql
care-attribution.sql
medication-attribution.sql
member-invitation.sql
invitation-issuing.sql
decisions-have-authors.sql
